    "result_text": "The full build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change\n\nThe specification: **a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.**\n\n`membershipLevel` is a derived field computed in `OwnerMapper`. I made two coordinated changes:\n\n**1. `OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 the cap itself**\n- Split the old points-based level computation into a private `uncappedMembershipLevel(owner)` helper.\n- `membershipLevel(owner)` now takes that uncapped level and caps it at `1 + max(uncappedMembershipLevel of every other household member)`. When the owner has no other household member, it returns the uncapped level unchanged.\n- Added `otherHouseholdMembers(owner)` \u2014 the owners sharing the same `householdId`, excluding the owner itself (by id).\n- The cap deliberately compares against the *uncapped* level of household members so that two mutual household members don't recurse into each other's caps.\n\n**2. `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` \u2014 allow household co-membership**\nThe accumulated `applyHousehold` rule rejected any second owner in an existing household with a 409 (`HouseholdDuplicateException`) unless `sharesHousehold=true`. The acceptance test creates two owners with the same lastName+postcode (same household) *without* that flag and requires both to be created successfully, then checks the second's level is at most the first's + 1. For the cap to be meaningful and testable, a household must be able to hold multiple members, so `applyHousehold` now simply assigns the derived `householdId` and admits the new member instead of rejecting it. Removed the now-unused `HouseholdDuplicateException` import.\n\n## Verification\n- `AcceptanceTest.coreCapsLevelByHousehold` passes. The audit log confirms the two owners get `membershipLevel=1` and `membershipLevel=2` (the second's uncapped level of 2 is exactly `1 + 1`, at the ceiling).\n- Full `./mvnw test`: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1 test run, 0 failures.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: making the test green required relaxing the pre-existing household-duplicate 409 rule, which goes beyond the literal one-line cap description. No remaining test asserts that 409, and the cap rule presupposes households with multiple members, so this is the coherent reading \u2014 but if that hard block was meant to stay, let me know and we can reconcile the two rules differently.",
